{
  "id": "iterm2",
  "name": "iTerm2",
  "kind": "app",
  "category": "Development",
  "match": {
    "macos_bundle": [
      "com.googlecode.iterm2"
    ],
    "window_title": []
  },
  "sources": [
    "https://iterm2.com/documentation-one-page.html",
    "https://iterm2.com/documentation-preferences-keys.html"
  ],
  "note": "From the official iTerm2 documentation (one-page manual, current as of iTerm2 3.6.x docs, fetched 2026-09-04). iTerm2's docs do not publish a keyboard-shortcuts cheat sheet; every chord here is one explicitly written out in prose in the General Usage, Highlights for New Users, Shell Integration, and Copy Mode sections, or (for tab/window selection) a direct enumeration of the documented Cmd+number / Cmd+Option+number pattern. Mouse-only bindings are omitted: cmd-click to open a URL/filename, option-drag to upload via scp, quad-click for smart selection, three-finger trackpad swipe/tap gestures, and middle-click to close a tab. Where the docs list several interchangeable keystrokes for one action (e.g. 'Esc, q, C-c, C-g' to exit Copy Mode, or 'h, Left arrow' to move left), only the first-listed keystroke is recorded as the action; the rest are equivalent alternates not listed separately. Two Copy Mode bindings use the literal vi characters '^' (start of indentation) and '$' (end of line), which the chord grammar cannot express (no caret or dollar-sign key token): for start-of-indentation the documented alternate M-m (Alt+M) is used instead, and 'Move to end of line ($)' is left out entirely since it has no alternate binding.",
